From: qwqgong-ui <noreply@qwqgong-ui.local>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 09:53:57 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] redirect: keep the redirect server off MPTCP

Go 1.24 turned MPTCP on by default for listeners, and the redirect server
takes the default because it never says otherwise. On an MPTCP socket
getsockopt(SOL_IP, SO_ORIGINAL_DST) answers EOPNOTSUPP, so loopIn() would
fail to resolve the destination and drop every connection an MPTCP client
opens through the redirect. Verified on Linux 7.2 with net.mptcp.enabled=1:
a plain accept returns the address, an MPTCP accept returns "operation not
supported".

Nothing about a redirect server benefits from multipath, so ask for it
explicitly rather than depending on the main module's go directive keeping
the GODEBUG at the old default.
---
 redirect_server.go | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/redirect_server.go b/redirect_server.go
index 754d757..dc81b7b 100644
--- a/redirect_server.go
+++ b/redirect_server.go
@@ -40,6 +40,10 @@ func (s *redirectServer) Start() error {
 	var listenConfig net.ListenConfig
 	// listenConfig.KeepAlive = C.TCPKeepAliveInitial
 	listenConfig.KeepAlive = 10 * time.Minute
+	// Since Go 1.24 listeners get MPTCP by default, and getsockopt(SO_ORIGINAL_DST)
+	// answers EOPNOTSUPP on an MPTCP socket, which would fail every redirected
+	// connection below. The redirect server has no use for multipath anyway.
+	listenConfig.SetMultipathTCP(false)
 	listener, err := listenConfig.Listen(s.ctx, M.NetworkFromNetAddr("tcp", s.listenAddr), M.SocksaddrFrom(s.listenAddr, 0).String())
 	if err != nil {
 		return err
